<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Leicester</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Leicester is a city in the East <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midlands">Midlands</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, on the River Soar. It began as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ratae-corieltauvorum">Ratae Corieltauvorum</a>, the Roman capital of the Corieltauvi, became an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> bishopric and one of the Five Boroughs of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danelaw">Danelaw</a>, and grew into a county and market town. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-iii-of-england">Richard III</a> was buried there after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-bosworth-field">Battle of Bosworth</a> in 1485, and his remains were rediscovered in 2012.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leicester.jpg" alt="Leicester" width="320" height="248" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NotFromUtrecht · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Leicester_Clock_Tower_wide_view.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After <b>Leicester</b> left in 1587 the States General stopped seeking a monarch and governed as a republic of seven provinces, Holland, Zeeland, Utrecht, Gelderland, Overijssel, Friesland and Groningen, with conquered lands to the south ruled in common as the Generality Lands.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The western half was then ruled by Æthelred, Lord of the Mercians, who accepted Alfred's overlordship and married Alfred's daughter Æthelflæd. After his death in 911 Æthelflæd ruled alone, built a chain of fortified burhs and recovered Derby and <b>Leicester</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-mercia">Kingdom of Mercia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Britain’s Anti-Slavery Society, founded in 1823, at first sought “mitigation and gradual abolition”; in 1824 Elizabeth Heyrick of <b>Leicester</b> demanded Immediate, Not Gradual Abolition, and the movement soon came round to her view.</blockquote><a href="/movement/abolitionism">Abolitionism · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>He often appears as Captain, General or King Ludd, and the legends place his home either in Anstey, near <b>Leicester</b>, or in Sherwood Forest.</blockquote><a href="/movement/luddites">Luddites · People</a></li><li><blockquote>Jacqueline Hirst and John Zavos record it being sung in places as far apart as New Delhi, Toledo and <b>Leicester</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/om-jai-jagdish-hare">Om Jai Jagdish Hare</a></li><li><blockquote>Thomas Wolsey, once Henry VIII's all-powerful Lord Chancellor, died at <b>Leicester</b> Abbey.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-29">29 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
